---
// About (English): the same position as /about. The definitions are a translation of the questionnaire text.
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { i18n } from '../../i18n';

const { link } = i18n(Astro);
---

<Layout title="About — Scientists Barometer" description="Reforms in science proceed without any assessment of their consequences. The project estimates how much time researchers really have left for research.">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Reform in Ukrainian science proceeds without any assessment of its consequences. Above all, the
    creation of unnecessary bureaucracy and the extra time researchers lose, which takes them away from
    their research, are ignored.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Quantity as the goal</h3>
    <p>
      The state of the research system is not assessed before or after reforms, and which changes they are
      meant to achieve is not defined. It looks as though the main goal of current reforms is to increase
      the number of publications, grant applications, characters in textbooks and so on. This can be seen
      from the rating methodologies of the state attestation and the National System of Researchers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Moreover, no one even asks what level of these indicators would satisfy the authors of the reforms.
      So the rule is always: the more, the better. But growth in quantitative indicators of this kind has a
      limit: potential harm to research quality through a lack of time and other resources. The more
      publications there are, the fewer resources each publication receives.
    </p>

    <h3>Aim of the project</h3>
    <p>
      The aim of the project is to try to estimate how much time researchers really have left for actually
      doing research. It is research that should accompany the preparation of publications and the
      fulfilment of other formal bureaucratic requirements, which are imposed directly or indirectly and
      which keep multiplying thanks to “reform”.
    </p>

    <h3 id="kategorii">Three categories of working time</h3>
    <p>
      The Law of Ukraine “On Scientific and Scientific-Technical Activity” defines research activity as
      intellectual creative activity aimed at obtaining new knowledge and (or) seeking ways to apply it.
      However, not everything researchers have to do is directly aimed at this, and part of what they do
      distracts from it. Working time is limited, so time for research shrinks as time spent on everything
      else grows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Researchers’ working time is therefore divided into three categories, in order to determine how much
      time really remains for actual scientific activity. The division adapts David Graeber’s concept of
      “bullshit jobs” to research work. The definitions below are translated from the questionnaire;
      respondents saw them in Ukrainian:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Actual scientific activity (ASA)</b>: activity that directly brings a researcher closer to
        creating new knowledge and (or) finding ways to apply it.</li>
      <li><b>Useful non-scientific activity (UNSA)</b>: activity that is not actually scientific, but often
        accompanies actual scientific activity and benefits the researcher and/or society.</li>
      <li><b>Meaningless activity (MA)</b>: activity that brings no benefit to the creation of a scientific
        result. There is no sense in researchers doing such work, but they are forced to do it by
        bureaucratic rules and by formal and informal requirements of governing and inspecting bodies at
        all levels. Doing it only distracts from and interferes with actual scientific work.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Author</h3>
    <p>
      Dmytro Maslov,<br />
      Senior Research Fellow<br />
      State Organization "Institute Of Market And Economic&amp;Ecological Researches of the National Academy
      of Sciences of Ukraine"<br />
      Contact: <a href={link('/participate')}>“Take part or leave feedback” form</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
